WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.667 --> 00:00:03.067
-Our first guest tonight is
an Academy Award-winning actor.

00:00:03.067 --> 00:00:04.767
He narrates
"Life on Our Planet,"

00:00:04.767 --> 00:00:06.200
which is streaming
now on Netflix.

00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:08.601
Please welcome back to the show
Morgan Freeman, everybody.

00:00:08.601 --> 00:00:10.601
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:10.601 --> 00:00:20.601
♪♪

00:00:20.601 --> 00:00:24.934
♪♪

00:00:24.934 --> 00:00:27.000
Welcome back!
-Thank you.

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:28.467
Nice to be back.
-It's nice to have you --

00:00:28.467 --> 00:00:30.467
It's nice to have you here,
back in New York City.

00:00:30.467 --> 00:00:31.567
-Thank you.
-When did you first

00:00:31.567 --> 00:00:34.501
come to the city?

00:00:34.501 --> 00:00:41.601
-I first came in
nineteen sixty...one, I think.

00:00:41.601 --> 00:00:43.667
-All right.
-'60, '60.

00:00:43.667 --> 00:00:46.367
1959 -- 1960.
-1960.

00:00:46.367 --> 00:00:48.801
-But I only stayed five months.
-Okay.

00:00:48.801 --> 00:00:51.501
-And then I fled.
[ Laughter ]

00:00:51.501 --> 00:00:53.400
And I went to San Francisco.
-Okay.

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:55.501
-Stayed in San Francisco
for about three years

00:00:55.501 --> 00:00:57.734
and then came back more ready.

00:00:57.734 --> 00:00:59.901
-Oh, okay.
So did you -- as an actor

00:00:59.901 --> 00:01:02.834
more ready or --
-Yep. As a performer.

00:01:02.834 --> 00:01:04.701
-Okay. Gotcha.
-'Cause I had taken dance.

00:01:04.701 --> 00:01:06.300
-Oh, that's right.
You did it all.

00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:08.067
Yeah, you did it all.
I remember we talked --

00:01:08.067 --> 00:01:09.767
You danced at the World's Fair
here in New York City.

00:01:09.767 --> 00:01:11.367
-Yeah, I --
[ Chuckles ]

00:01:11.367 --> 00:01:13.467
[ Laughter ]

00:01:13.467 --> 00:01:14.534
You know that?

00:01:14.534 --> 00:01:15.968
-I mean, you're the only person

00:01:15.968 --> 00:01:18.167
I've ever met who's danced
at a World's Fair.

00:01:18.167 --> 00:01:22.400
-Right. 1984 World's Fair,
New York world's fair.

00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:24.400
-That's amazing.
So, you come back in 1963,

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:25.734
you're a performer.
-Yeah.

00:01:25.734 --> 00:01:28.901
-And how did you --
how did you find New York City?

00:01:28.901 --> 00:01:31.734
-In 1963?
-Yeah.

00:01:31.734 --> 00:01:34.734
-Well, it's probably the most
exciting place, you know,

00:01:34.734 --> 00:01:40.801
particularly that I was ready to
make the move into the big-time.

00:01:40.801 --> 00:01:42.467
By that, I mean get a job.

00:01:42.467 --> 00:01:44.767
[ Laughter ]

00:01:44.767 --> 00:01:46.601
-Just a paying job?
-Right.

00:01:46.601 --> 00:01:49.968
-And now do you was -- your
first New York City apartment --

00:01:49.968 --> 00:01:51.667
Probably not a penthouse
with a beautiful view

00:01:51.667 --> 00:01:53.767
of the park or anything.
-No, no, no.

00:01:53.767 --> 00:01:59.701
I had one-room
in a place on 12th Street

00:01:59.701 --> 00:02:04.467
and Avenue A,
called Paradise Alley.

00:02:04.467 --> 00:02:07.400
-All right. And was it -- was it
more Paradise or more Alley?

00:02:07.400 --> 00:02:09.033
[ Laughter ]

00:02:09.033 --> 00:02:11.701
-It was enough of both
to get the name.

00:02:11.701 --> 00:02:13.767
-There you go.

00:02:13.767 --> 00:02:19.767
-A lot of young dancers,
singers, musicians,

00:02:19.767 --> 00:02:22.801
actors lived there.
And it wasn't --

00:02:22.801 --> 00:02:26.767
It wasn't subsidized.
It was just affordable.

00:02:26.767 --> 00:02:29.167
-You were doing --
How long in New York

00:02:29.167 --> 00:02:32.067
before you did "Hello, Dolly!"
on Broadway?

00:02:32.067 --> 00:02:34.467
-I did "Hello, Dolly!"
on Broadway.

00:02:34.467 --> 00:02:37.667
I got the job in November '67.
-Mm-hmm.

00:02:37.667 --> 00:02:40.300
-And I worked there
for 11 months.

00:02:40.300 --> 00:02:41.767
-And was that
one of the first,

00:02:41.767 --> 00:02:43.367
"I think I've made it"
moments for you?

00:02:43.367 --> 00:02:44.501
-Yeah, absolutely.

00:02:44.501 --> 00:02:46.167
-And was doing a show
for 11 months,

00:02:46.167 --> 00:02:48.234
was it exciting the whole time?

00:02:48.234 --> 00:02:50.100
-I was working
with Pearl Bailey.

00:02:50.100 --> 00:02:52.601
-There you go.
-Every night was a joy.

00:02:52.601 --> 00:02:54.200
-That's pretty exciting.
-Yeah.

00:02:54.200 --> 00:02:56.501
-You've also -- you also
had shows that had

00:02:56.501 --> 00:02:58.501
shorter runs on Broadway.
-[ Chuckles ]

00:02:58.501 --> 00:03:02.234
-What was your show
that only had nine performances?

00:03:02.234 --> 00:03:04.000
-"The Mighty Jets."
-"The Mighty Jets."

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:05.434
-Yeah.
-Did you have any sense

00:03:05.434 --> 00:03:08.901
on the first night that it would
only be there for nine nights?

00:03:08.901 --> 00:03:11.868
-I had that sense
while we were rehearsing.

00:03:11.868 --> 00:03:15.901
[ Laughter ]

00:03:15.901 --> 00:03:17.334
That's the truth.
-Yeah.

00:03:17.334 --> 00:03:19.601
So kind of sometimes you know?
-Yeah, sometimes, you know,

00:03:19.601 --> 00:03:21.734
"This is not going to work."

00:03:21.734 --> 00:03:24.100
-Yeah. Now, an interesting
thing happened, though,

00:03:24.100 --> 00:03:26.100
because the show
only had nine performances,

00:03:26.100 --> 00:03:28.234
but you received
a Tony nomination.

00:03:28.234 --> 00:03:30.801
-Yes.
-Is that sort of cold comfort

00:03:30.801 --> 00:03:32.467
when you receive praise

00:03:32.467 --> 00:03:34.367
for a show
that had such a short run?

00:03:34.367 --> 00:03:36.534
-It is. Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:03:36.534 --> 00:03:38.601
-Operative word,
however, is comfort.

00:03:38.601 --> 00:03:40.868
-Uh-huh.
-Yeah. I mean, it's like,

00:03:40.868 --> 00:03:42.868
"Okay."
[ Laughter ]

00:03:42.868 --> 00:03:44.367
-Who decided --

00:03:44.367 --> 00:03:47.167
Was it something you decided,
"I'm going to be a performer,"

00:03:47.167 --> 00:03:48.934
or was it decided for you?

00:03:48.934 --> 00:03:50.601
-It was decided for me.
-Yeah? How about?

00:03:50.601 --> 00:03:52.567
-I was born.
-How long? How old were you?

00:03:52.567 --> 00:03:54.200
-I was born.
-You were born, yeah.

00:03:54.200 --> 00:03:57.200
[ Laughter ]

00:03:57.200 --> 00:03:58.467
-Yeah.
-That's a fact.

00:03:58.467 --> 00:04:00.234
-Yeah.
-Sorry.

00:04:00.234 --> 00:04:02.534
-Did you know -- at what age
did you think,

00:04:02.534 --> 00:04:04.901
"Oh, I'm gonna be better
at this than other things"?

00:04:04.901 --> 00:04:09.200
-I don't know.
-Yeah.

00:04:09.200 --> 00:04:14.601
-I just was always able.
I always went whole hog.

00:04:14.601 --> 00:04:17.200
-Mm-hmm.

00:04:17.200 --> 00:04:21.868
-When we played --
Like, I had a friend

00:04:21.868 --> 00:04:25.434
when I was like 5, 6.

00:04:25.434 --> 00:04:27.567
And we played, like,
horse and buggy.

00:04:27.567 --> 00:04:32.033
You just put a clothesline,
a rope, around your waist,

00:04:32.033 --> 00:04:34.167
and he holds on to it
and goes -- [ Clicks tongue ]

00:04:34.167 --> 00:04:37.701
[ Laughter ]

00:04:37.701 --> 00:04:39.834
-It's still -- sometimes kids
who have the talent

00:04:39.834 --> 00:04:44.901
don't have the confidence
to then go do a school play

00:04:44.901 --> 00:04:46.501
or join the theater club.
Was that something --

00:04:46.501 --> 00:04:48.534
As soon as you got to school --
-I had a mother

00:04:48.534 --> 00:04:51.167
who was very into --
-Oh, that helps.

00:04:51.167 --> 00:04:56.033
-Yeah.
And I had three brothers

00:04:56.033 --> 00:04:59.234
who -- and we were all,
on some level,

00:04:59.234 --> 00:05:02.100
artistic, you know?

00:05:02.100 --> 00:05:03.601
-We're gonna take
a quick break

00:05:03.601 --> 00:05:06.567
and then talk about this Netflix
documentary you're narrating.

00:05:06.567 --> 00:05:08.167
But I wonder,
you get asked so often,

00:05:08.167 --> 00:05:11.267
what's the worst thing
you've been asked to narrate?

00:05:11.267 --> 00:05:16.234
[ Laughter ]

00:05:16.234 --> 00:05:18.701
-I mean, you know,
somebody's telephone number.

00:05:18.701 --> 00:05:20.267
-Oh, people ask you
to leave messages and stuff?

00:05:20.267 --> 00:05:21.400
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:05:21.400 --> 00:05:23.133
I don't --
You don't seem to me

00:05:23.133 --> 00:05:26.300
like the kind of guy
who would like doing that.

00:05:26.300 --> 00:05:30.033
[ Laughter ]

00:05:30.033 --> 00:05:31.868
-Don't -- Don't -- Don't --
Don't get me into trouble.

00:05:31.868 --> 00:05:33.467
[ Laughter ]

00:05:33.467 --> 00:05:35.801
-We'll be right back with more
from Morgan Freeman.

